  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. West's lead is won by South. The contract will make easily if either diamonds or clubs can be established before the defense can clear the spades.\n\nSouth can almost always make the contract with proper technique. He should begin by leading a diamond towards the dummy. If West follows, the contract is certain. South simply ducks in dummy. If East discards, South can take the marked finesse in diamonds on the second round.\n\nIf West plays the ♦Q on the first round, South still must duck in dummy. West may be falsecarding from 4 diamonds to the ♦Q.\n\nIf West discards on the first round of diamonds, East will have 2 certain stoppers. South should win in dummy and play on clubs. If clubs are 3-2, the contract still will make.\n\nIt takes a combination of 4-1 clubs and East having all 4 diamonds to defeat this line of play. An equally effective line of play would be to cash the ♣A and ♣K before playing diamonds. If clubs are 3-2, a trick can be surrendered in clubs. If clubs break badly, South can play diamonds as before.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. West's lead is won by South. The contract will make easily if either diamonds or clubs can be established before the defense can clear the spades.",
    "South can almost always make the contract with proper technique. He should begin by leading a diamond towards the dummy. If West follows, the contract is certain. South simply ducks in dummy. If East discards, South can take the marked finesse in diamonds on the second round.",
